  "tail": "### `thm-compact-iff-fip` — CERTIFY\n\nCURRENT TEXT READ: \"In practice the second is easier to apply, because a nested family of nonempty closed sets has the finite intersection property for free, and the theorem then produces a point lying in all of them at once. That is how it is used below in [[thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff]], whose step 7.1 turns a decreasing sequence of nonempty closed sets into a point common to all of them. [[thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space]] uses the theorem in the opposite direction: from a family of closed sets whose intersection is empty it extracts a finite subfamily whose intersection is already empty.\"\n\nASSESSMENT: Both attributions check out against the cited files as they stand on disk.\n\n- `thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff.md` step 6.1 establishes the family decreases: \"$\\overline{V_{n+1}} \\subseteq V_n \\subseteq \\overline{V_n}$.\" Step 7.1 then reads: \"The family $\\{\\overline{V_n} : n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}$ of closed subsets of the compact space $\\overline{V_0}$ has the finite intersection property... So [L5] gives a point $x \\in \\bigcap_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\overline{V_n}$,\" where [L5] is \"A space is compact exactly when every family of its closed subsets with the finite intersection property has nonempty intersection ([[thm-compact-iff-fip]]).\" That is exactly \"a decreasing sequence of nonempty closed sets into a point common to all of them\" — the point-producing direction, correctly attributed.\n- `thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space.md` [L8] states the same theorem; its sole invocation is step 4.1: \"By [L8] the family $\\mathcal{F}$ therefore fails the finite intersection property, so some finite list in it has empty intersection... $K := K_0 \\cap \\dots \\cap K_n$...\" fed by step 3.1: \"Its intersection is $Q \\setminus (U \\cup V)$ by [L1], which is empty by step 2.1.\" That is precisely \"from a family of closed sets whose intersection is empty it extracts a finite subfamily whose intersection is already empty\" — the contrapositive direction, correctly attributed. `grep` for `L8` in that file confirms exactly one use, at step 4.1, so \"uses the theorem in the opposite direction\" is not overstating a family with mixed uses.\n- Both named items are strictly later than `thm-compact-iff-fip` in `library/topology/compactness.md`'s item list (lines 6, 15, 19), so \"used below\" is the correct page-scoped reading order.\n- `grep -rl thm-compact-iff-fip items/` finds three other citers: `rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger.md` (merely lists it in a \"theorems of ZF\" catalog, not a use), `lem-compact-uniform-spaces-are-complete.md` and `thm-compactness-via-nets-filters-and-ultrafilters.md` — the latter two do use the point-producing direction, but they live on `uniform-spaces.md` and `nets-and-filters.md` respectively, not on `compactness.md`. The sentence's \"below\" is page-scoped, so these do not make it incomplete, let alone false.\n\nCOLLATERAL: none found. Statement, title, `deps`, Facts & Assumptions, and all four numbered proof steps (1.1–4.1) are untouched and remain correct and correctly cited on inspection. The other two Remarks are unmodified by this repair and are not implicated by the named defect.\n\nSTAMPS: `verification:` currently holds only `precheck: pass` — no `judge` block and no `audited`/`verified` stamp. That is the correct state per the dispatch: the stale `z-ai/glm-5.2` judge block and the obsolete `claude-fable-5` page-scope `verified` stamp were properly removed as a material repair, and no new stamp has been written yet pending this certification.\n\nN certified, M refused: **1 certified, 0 refused**"
